1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:01,844
Imagine your boss says,

2
00:00:01,974 --> 00:00:03,745
build me a fast online shop.

3
00:00:04,295 --> 00:00:05,233
Sounds simple.

4
00:00:05,783 --> 00:00:07,170
But what does fast mean?

5
00:00:07,720 --> 00:00:10,429
One developer hears: pages load in a blink.

6
00:00:10,979 --> 00:00:13,411
Another hears: orders ship the same day.

7
00:00:13,961 --> 00:00:15,199
Both build something.

8
00:00:15,749 --> 00:00:17,775
And neither is what the owner wanted.

9
00:00:18,825 --> 00:00:20,745
This is why requirements exist.

10
00:00:21,295 --> 00:00:25,541
In this lesson, you will write requirements that everyone reads the same way.

11
00:00:27,091 --> 00:00:29,011
First, ask who needs the system.

12
00:00:29,561 --> 00:00:31,524
These people are called stakeholders.

13
00:00:32,074 --> 00:00:33,332
The owner wants sales.

14
00:00:33,882 --> 00:00:35,696
Shoppers want it quick, and easy.

15
00:00:36,246 --> 00:00:38,507
Support staff want clear order records.

16
00:00:39,057 --> 00:00:42,428
The operations team wants something they can run, and fix.

17
00:00:42,978 --> 00:00:44,834
Each group cares about different things.

18
00:00:45,384 --> 00:00:48,626
So a good requirement starts with a conversation, not a guess.

19
00:00:50,676 --> 00:00:53,428
Teams often write requirements as user stories.

20
00:00:53,978 --> 00:00:55,791
A user story has three parts:

21
00:00:56,341 --> 00:00:57,003
who,

22
00:00:57,133 --> 00:00:57,751
what,

23
00:00:57,881 --> 00:00:58,692
and why.

24
00:00:59,242 --> 00:01:00,245
As a shopper,

25
00:01:00,375 --> 00:01:02,081
I want to search for a product,

26
00:01:02,211 --> 00:01:03,790
so that I can find it fast.

27
00:01:04,340 --> 00:01:05,385
The why matters.

28
00:01:05,935 --> 00:01:08,815
It tells developers what the feature is really for.

29
00:01:10,865 --> 00:01:12,572
A story says what is wanted.

30
00:01:13,122 --> 00:01:16,044
Acceptance criteria say how we know it is done.

31
00:01:16,594 --> 00:01:19,538
A popular way to write them is: given, when, then.

32
00:01:20,088 --> 00:01:21,987
Given a shopper on the product page,

33
00:01:22,117 --> 00:01:23,717
when they search for the word mug,

34
00:01:23,847 --> 00:01:25,938
then they see only products named mug.

35
00:01:26,488 --> 00:01:29,368
Now anyone can check it, and a test can automate it.

36
00:01:29,918 --> 00:01:32,734
If you cannot check it, it is not a requirement yet.

37
00:01:34,784 --> 00:01:35,915
Now, the how well.

38
00:01:36,465 --> 00:01:39,345
Words like fast, easy, and secure are traps.

39
00:01:39,895 --> 00:01:43,201
They sound clear, but they mean different things to different people.

40
00:01:43,751 --> 00:01:45,138
So make them measurable.

41
00:01:45,688 --> 00:01:49,187
Fast becomes: the product list loads in under one second.

42
00:01:49,737 --> 00:01:53,299
Secure becomes: passwords are never stored as plain text.

43
00:01:53,849 --> 00:01:56,623
Now there is a number to check, and a rule to test.

44
00:01:58,673 --> 00:02:00,529
You cannot build everything at once.

45
00:02:01,079 --> 00:02:02,508
So we rank requirements.

46
00:02:03,058 --> 00:02:05,106
A simple method is called MoSCoW.

47
00:02:05,656 --> 00:02:06,446
Must have,

48
00:02:06,576 --> 00:02:07,408
should have,

49
00:02:07,538 --> 00:02:08,306
could have,

50
00:02:08,436 --> 00:02:09,908
and will not have, for now.

51
00:02:10,458 --> 00:02:12,954
For CloudCart, placing an order is a must.

52
00:02:13,504 --> 00:02:15,210
A wish list is only a could.

53
00:02:15,760 --> 00:02:18,470
Ranking keeps the first release small, and shippable.

54
00:02:20,520 --> 00:02:23,080
Good requirements connect to the rest of the cycle.

55
00:02:23,630 --> 00:02:25,955
Remember this rule from the CloudCart code?

56
00:02:26,505 --> 00:02:28,511
Quantity must be one to a hundred.

57
00:02:29,061 --> 00:02:31,941
A rule like this should start as a written requirement.

58
00:02:32,491 --> 00:02:35,840
Design plans for it, code enforces it, and tests check it.

59
00:02:36,390 --> 00:02:39,441
When a requirement changes, you know what else must change.

60
00:02:41,491 --> 00:02:43,347
Watch for four common mistakes.

61
00:02:43,897 --> 00:02:45,177
One: vague words.

62
00:02:45,727 --> 00:02:47,882
Two: no owner, to make the decision.

63
00:02:48,432 --> 00:02:52,080
Three: listing features, but forgetting how well they must work.

64
00:02:52,630 --> 00:02:54,806
Four: changing requirements silently.

65
00:02:55,356 --> 00:02:57,617
Write changes down, and tell everyone.

66
00:02:59,667 --> 00:03:00,542
Quick check.

67
00:03:01,092 --> 00:03:03,012
What three parts make a user story?

68
00:03:03,562 --> 00:03:05,631
What do given, when, and then describe?

69
00:03:06,181 --> 00:03:08,869
How do you turn the word fast into something you can test?

70
00:03:09,419 --> 00:03:11,488
Pause the video, and answer out loud.

71
00:03:18,038 --> 00:03:19,702
Next lesson: design.

72
00:03:20,252 --> 00:03:22,130
Subscribe, so you do not miss it.
